        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan on <strong class="text-white">30 to 60 minutes</strong> for lightweight spackle in a nail hole, <strong class="text-white">one to two hours</strong> for standard vinyl, and <strong class="text-white">overnight</strong> for anything approaching half an inch. Humidity and a cold room can double every one of those figures.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then stop watching the clock and use the temperature test: if the patch feels cooler than the wall beside it, water is still leaving and it is not ready. Fill thin, wait until it matches, sand at 180 to 220 grit, and prime before you paint. That sequence is the whole difference between a repair nobody can find and one you notice every time the afternoon light crosses the wall.
          </p>
